window.__hvBdRegister("plut-lives-3", {"key":"plut-lives-3","sections":[{"i":0,"j":14,"loc":"Life of Pyrrhus, chs. 1–9","title":"Pyrrhus's rescue, exile, and return to Epeirus","summary":"Plutarch traces the Epeirot kings from Neoptolemus son of Achilles down to Aeacides, father of Pyrrhus. When Aeacides is driven out, the infant Pyrrhus is carried across a flooded river and taken in by Glaucias the Illyrian, who restores him to the throne at twelve. Driven out again at seventeen, Pyrrhus fights beside Demetrius at Ipsus, goes to Egypt as a hostage, and marries Antigone, stepdaughter of Ptolemy. Back in Epeirus he shares the throne with Neoptolemus until he learns of a poison plot and kills him. He then takes land in Macedonia, defeats Demetrius's general Pantauchus in single combat, and is likened to Alexander; the section ends with sketches of his character and his several marriages.","ideas":["The infant Pyrrhus's escape and his supplication at the knees of Glaucias","The plot of Gelon and Neoptolemus, exposed by an overheard conversation","The duel with Pantauchus and the Macedonians' comparison of Pyrrhus to Alexander","Generalship as the one kingly study; mildness toward friends and critics"],"terms":[{"t":"Aeacidae","d":"The royal line of Epeirus, which claimed descent from Achilles and through him from Aeacus."}]},{"i":15,"j":29,"loc":"Life of Pyrrhus, chs. 10–14","title":"Macedonia won and lost; Tarentum calls","summary":"Hailed as 'Eagle' by the Epeirots, Pyrrhus raids Macedonia, makes peace with Demetrius, and then, urged by the other kings, attacks him again. A dream of Alexander comes before the march, the Macedonian army deserts Demetrius at Beroea, and Pyrrhus is proclaimed king, only to share the country with Lysimachus. Lysimachus later turns the Macedonians against him as a foreigner, and Pyrrhus loses the kingdom as fast as he gained it; Plutarch comments on how kings treat war and peace as coins to be spent. Tarentum, at war with Rome, invites him over the protest of Meton. The section ends with Cineas's famous dialogue, which asks what Pyrrhus will do after conquering everything and shows the quiet he hopes for is already his.","ideas":["Kings' greed knows no limits, and peoples learn faithlessness from them","Pyrrhus is proclaimed king of Macedonia without a battle, then driven out","Meton's mock revel warns the Tarentines they will lose their freedom","Cineas questions whether endless conquest has any purpose"],"terms":[{"t":"Cineas","d":"A Thessalian orator, pupil of Demosthenes, who served Pyrrhus as envoy and adviser."}]},{"i":30,"j":44,"loc":"Life of Pyrrhus, chs. 15–21","title":"Heracleia, the Roman embassies, and Asculum","summary":"A storm scatters Pyrrhus's fleet, and he swims ashore in Messapia. At Tarentum he shuts the gymnasia and drafts the citizens. At the river Siris he admires the Roman camp's order. He survives an attack by Oplax and an exchange of armour that almost costs him the battle, and his elephants win the day against Laevinus at heavy cost. Cineas goes to Rome with peace terms; the blind Appius Claudius has himself carried into the senate and shames it into refusing. Fabricius turns down Pyrrhus's gold, is unmoved by an elephant, and later warns Pyrrhus of his physician's offer to poison him. After the hard battle at Asculum Pyrrhus remarks that one more such victory will ruin him.","ideas":["Pyrrhus finds 'barbarian' Roman discipline not barbarous","Appius Claudius's speech ends the peace talks","Fabricius's incorruptibility and the exposed poison plot","Victories that drain Pyrrhus while Rome refills its legions as from a fountain"],"terms":[{"t":"Pyrrhic victory","d":"A win bought at such cost that it ruins the victor; it comes from Pyrrhus's remark after Asculum."}]},{"i":45,"j":55,"loc":"Life of Pyrrhus, chs. 22–26","title":"Sicily, Beneventum, and the return to Macedonia","summary":"Offered both Sicily and Macedonia, Pyrrhus chooses Sicily. He drives back the Carthaginians, is the first up the wall at Eryx, and defeats the Mamertines. His harsh levying of oarsmen and the killing of Thoenon turn the Sicilian cities against him, and he goes back to Italy, cutting a Mamertine champion in two on the way. At Beneventum a night march loses its way, and Manius Curius routs his army as the elephants turn on their own lines. Plutarch sums up six wasted years with Antigonus's image of a dice-player who throws well but cannot use his throws. Back in Epeirus, Pyrrhus wins over Antigonus's infantry and dedicates Gallic shields to Athena Itonis. His Gauls then plunder the royal tombs at Aegae, and he soon accepts an invitation from Cleonymus of Sparta.","ideas":["Pyrrhus turns from popular leader to tyrant in Sicily","His parting remark about a wrestling-ground for Carthage and Rome","Beneventum gives Rome mastery of Italy","Brilliant at winning, unable to secure what he won"],"terms":[]},{"i":56,"j":73,"loc":"Life of Pyrrhus, chs. 26–34","title":"The assault on Sparta and death at Argos","summary":"Cleonymus, slighted at home and in his marriage to Chilonis, brings Pyrrhus against Sparta. Pyrrhus delays the attack for a night, and the Spartan women help dig a trench against the elephants. Acrotatus drives back Ptolemy's Gauls, and Pyrrhus's horse is killed under him at the trench; reinforcements under Ameinias and King Areus save the city. Pyrrhus's son Ptolemy falls in a rearguard action, and Pyrrhus avenges him on the Spartans. Called into Argos by Aristeas, he enters at night, sees a bronze wolf fighting a bull that matches an oracle of his death, and is trapped in the narrow streets as Helenus's troops and a wounded elephant block the gate. An old woman's roof-tile stuns him, Zopyrus cuts off his head, and Antigonus weeps at the sight and buries him with honour.","ideas":["The Spartan women's part in defending the city","The dream of thunderbolts and Lysimachus's reading of it","The wolf-and-bull oracle at Argos","Antigonus's grief and mildness to Helenus"],"terms":[]},{"i":74,"j":84,"loc":"Life of Marius, chs. 1–7","title":"Marius's origins, character, and early career","summary":"Plutarch starts with Roman naming customs, prompted by Marius having no third name. From a statue he describes Marius as harsh and warlike, and he regrets that Marius scorned Greek learning and never sacrificed to the Muses and Graces. Born to poor parents near Arpinum, Marius serves under Scipio at Numantia, and Scipio points to him as a possible successor. As tribune he faces down the consul Cotta and Metellus over a voting law, yet opposes a grain dole. He loses two aedileships in one day, barely escapes a bribery conviction for the praetorship, clears brigands in Spain, and marries Julia, aunt of Caesar. As Metellus's legate against Jugurtha, he shares the soldiers' toils and wins their devotion.","ideas":["His harsh nature is untempered by Greek culture","Scipio's 'Here, perhaps' raises his hopes","His tribunate offends both senate and people","Soldiers love a general who shares their labours"],"terms":[{"t":"curule chair","d":"The seat of the higher magistrates; holding such an office freed a man and his descendants from a patron."}]},{"i":85,"j":101,"loc":"Life of Marius, chs. 8–14","title":"Rivalry with Metellus, consulship, and the Cimbri","summary":"Marius hounds Metellus's friend Turpillius to death on a false charge, sails home, slanders his commander, and is elected consul. He enrols poor men without property and taunts the nobles about their ancestors. He takes over Metellus's army, but Sulla, his quaestor, receives Jugurtha's surrender from Bocchus, the first seed of their deadly hatred. News of the Cimbri and Teutones, whose origins Plutarch weighs, brings Marius a second consulship. He leads Jugurtha in triumph, and Jugurtha dies in the Roman prison. He hardens his troops into 'Marian mules', rewards Trebonius for killing the officer who tried to violate him, and through Saturninus wins a fourth consulship.","ideas":["Ingratitude toward Metellus raises Marius to power","The Jugurtha seal-ring starts the feud with Sulla","The Cimbri as possible Cimmerians from the far north","Strict justice makes his harshness acceptable to his soldiers"],"terms":[{"t":"Marian mules","d":"A nickname for soldiers who carried their own baggage and worked uncomplainingly under Marius's training."}]},{"i":102,"j":117,"loc":"Life of Marius, chs. 15–23","title":"Aquae Sextiae and Catulus's retreat","summary":"Marius digs a canal at the Rhone mouth and keeps his men inside their camp so they grow used to the Teutones' looks and cries, despite their complaints. He consults the prophetess Martha, and portents and a pair of ringed vultures promise victory. The Teutones take six days to march past his camp, and he follows them to Aquae Sextiae. A skirmish over water turns into a rout of the Ambrones, whose women attack fugitives and pursuers alike. Two days later Marcellus's ambush in the rear completes the destruction of the Teutones. News of a fifth consulship reaches Marius at the victory pyre. In the north, meanwhile, the Cimbri slide down the Alps on their shields, and Catulus leads his panicking army's retreat himself so the disgrace falls on him rather than on Rome.","ideas":["Getting used to a fearsome enemy takes away the terror","The Ambrones' war-cry is answered by the Ligurians","Bones fence Massalian vineyards; rain follows great battles","Fortune mixes every success with evil"],"terms":[]},{"i":118,"j":127,"loc":"Life of Marius, chs. 24–28","title":"Vercellae and the bought sixth consulship","summary":"Marius puts off his triumph, joins Catulus, and mocks the Cimbri's envoys by producing the captive kings of the Teutones. He alters the javelin so its shaft bends on impact and agrees with Boeorix to fight on the plain of Vercellae. In Sulla's account, Marius loses the enemy in the dust while Catulus's men bear the fighting, and heat and dust help the Romans. The Cimbrian women kill their own men and children and then themselves. Catulus's men claim the victory, but the people hail Marius as third founder of Rome, and he shares the triumph. Hungry for a sixth consulship, he courts the crowd, joins Saturninus and Glaucia, and, according to Rutilius, buys the office.","ideas":["The dispute over credit between Marius and Catulus","The horrific end of the Cimbrian camp","Bold in battle, timid before the assembly","He prefers being the greatest to being the best"],"terms":[]},{"i":128,"j":135,"loc":"Life of Marius, chs. 29–34","title":"Saturninus, Metellus's exile, and Marius's decline","summary":"Marius says in the senate that he will not swear to Saturninus's law, so as to trap Metellus, then takes the oath himself. Metellus alone refuses, says acting honourably in danger is the mark of a good man, and goes into exile. Marius plays nobles and Saturninus against each other, then crushes the rising, which leaves him hated by both sides. He opposes Metellus's recall and goes to Asia, where he tells Mithridates to be stronger than Rome or obey. Neglected in peacetime, he nearly comes to blows with Sulla over Bocchus's statues, then commands sluggishly in the Social War. In old age he exercises in the Campus Martius to win the Mithridatic command, and the better citizens pity his greed.","ideas":["Marius treats deceit as a talent; Metellus holds to his word","Marius is fit for war but useless in peace","The Social War raises Sulla and diminishes Marius","Ambition that no good fortune can satisfy"],"terms":[{"t":"interdiction from fire and water","d":"A Roman sentence of banishment that denied the condemned shelter and necessities."}]},{"i":136,"j":151,"loc":"Life of Marius, chs. 35–40","title":"Flight from Sulla: Minturnae and Carthage","summary":"Sulpicius has the Mithridatic command voted to Marius, and Sulla marches on Rome and drives Marius out. Young Marius escapes hidden in a cart of beans. The elder Marius is blown ashore near Circeii, wanders hungry, and recalls the boyhood omen of an eagle's nest with seven chicks. Sailors abandon him at the Liris; he hides in a marsh but is caught naked and taken to Minturnae, where he is lodged with Fannia, whom he had once fined. A Gallic or Cimbrian soldier sent to kill him flees, crying that he cannot kill Caius Marius, and the townspeople send him off by sea. Turned away from Africa, he answers that he sits a fugitive amid the ruins of Carthage. He and his son, freed by a concubine's love, barely escape Iampsas's horsemen.","ideas":["The omen of seven eaglets and seven consulships","The ass that turns to the water as a sign of escape by sea","The executioner who cannot kill Marius","Marius and Carthage as fellow ruins of fortune"],"terms":[]},{"i":152,"j":165,"loc":"Life of Marius, chs. 41–46","title":"Return with Cinna, the massacres, and death","summary":"Marius lands in Etruria, frees slaves, joins Cinna, and starves Rome by seizing the grain-ships and Ostia. Octavius sticks to the law and to his seers and is killed on the rostra. Marius pretends to wait for a vote recalling him, then enters with his slave bodyguard, the Bardyaei, who kill anyone he does not greet. Cornutus's slaves save their master with a disguised corpse, while the orator Antonius is betrayed by a wine-seller and Catulus suffocates himself. Elected consul a seventh time, Marius is terrified by Sulla's approach, drinks to escape sleepless nights, and dies seventeen days into office still craving the Mithridatic command. Plutarch contrasts him with Plato, who was grateful for what he had, and closes with the cruelty and suicide of the younger Marius.","ideas":["Octavius's legalism and superstition bring his ruin","Massacre signalled by a withheld greeting","Loyalty and betrayal: Cornutus, Antonius, Catulus","Gratitude for present goods against endless hope for more"],"terms":[{"t":"Bardyaei","d":"Marius's bodyguard of runaway slaves, finally killed in their sleep by Cinna and Sertorius."}]},{"i":166,"j":181,"loc":"Life of Lysander, chs. 1–9","title":"Lysander's rise and his courting of Cyrus","summary":"Plutarch opens with Lysander's statue at Delphi and his Spartan upbringing: poor, ambitious, incorruptible himself, yet the man who filled Sparta with gold. Sent out as admiral, he builds up Ephesus, wins the young prince Cyrus by deference and gets an extra obol for his sailors, and defeats Antiochus, Alcibiades' pilot, at Notium. He plants the seeds of oligarchic clubs in the cities and undermines his upright successor Callicratidas, who refuses to fawn on Persians and dies at Arginusae. Reappointed in effect as vice-admiral, Lysander shows his taste for deceit at Miletus, and with Cyrus' money he takes Lampsacus as the Athenian fleet gathers at Aegospotami.","ideas":["Lysander personally despises money but brings wealth and the love of it into Sparta","The extra obol from Cyrus drains the Athenian fleet of rowers","Callicratidas' Doric honesty is contrasted with Lysander's craft","Patching the lion's skin with the fox's: deceit as a method of war"],"terms":[{"t":"decadarchy","d":"Rule of a city by a board of ten partisans, which Lysander's political clubs prepared for."},{"t":"nauarchos (admiral)","d":"The Spartan fleet command, which by law no man could hold twice, so Lysander went out nominally as vice-admiral."}]},{"i":182,"j":197,"loc":"Life of Lysander, chs. 10–15","title":"Aegospotami and the surrender of Athens","summary":"For four days Lysander keeps his fleet in line without fighting while the Athenians grow careless; Alcibiades' warning is rejected. On the fifth day a raised shield signals the attack and the Athenian fleet is taken almost empty, Conon alone escaping; one hour ends the long war. Plutarch digresses on the Dioscuri and the stone that fell from heaven at Aegospotami, weighing Anaxagoras and Daïmachus. Philocles and three thousand prisoners are executed, decadarchies and harmosts are installed, and Athens, starved out, accepts the ephors' terms; the walls are pulled down to flute music and the Thirty are set up.","ideas":["Feigned inaction lures the Athenians into a fatal routine","A single hour ends the Peloponnesian War","Meteorite of Aegospotami and rival theories of falling stars","A chorus from Euripides' Electra moves the allies to spare Athens"],"terms":[{"t":"harmost","d":"A Spartan governor placed over a subject city alongside Lysander's ten rulers."},{"t":"the Thirty","d":"The oligarchic board Lysander set over Athens after its surrender."}]},{"i":198,"j":213,"loc":"Life of Lysander, chs. 16–22.2","title":"Gold, divine honours, and recall to Sparta","summary":"Gylippus steals from the treasure Lysander sends home and is betrayed by the riddle of owls under the tiles; Sparta debates banning coin and permits it only for public use, a policy Plutarch criticises. Lysander sets up statues at Delphi, receives altars and paeans as a god, and patronises poets. His cruelty grows: eight hundred Milesian democrats are lured out on oath and slaughtered. Pharnabazus' complaints bring a recall by scytale, and Pharnabazus tricks him with a swapped letter; Lysander leaves for Ammon, returns to back the Thirty, and is checked by King Pausanias.","ideas":["Public honour of money corrupts private morals more than law can prevent","Lysander is the first Greek honoured with altars as a god","The scytale as Sparta's secret dispatch system","Pharnabazus outwits Lysander, 'playing the Cretan against a Cretan'"],"terms":[{"t":"skytalē (scytale)","d":"A staff around which a strip of parchment was wound so that a message could be read only on its twin staff."}]},{"i":214,"j":239,"loc":"Life of Lysander, chs. 22.3–30","title":"Agesilaus, the secret plot, and Haliartus","summary":"Lysander secures the kingship for Agesilaus over Leotychides by reinterpreting the oracle about a lame reign, but in Asia the jealous king humiliates him by making him carver of meats. Embittered, Lysander plots to open the kingship to all Heracleidae or all Spartans, using a speech by Cleon of Halicarnassus and a staged scheme involving Silenus, a supposed son of Apollo, which fails through one accomplice's cowardice. Turning against Thebes, he invades Boeotia and is killed before Haliartus, fulfilling an oracle about the Hoplites and a dragon. His poverty, revealed at death, wins him honour, and the suitors who abandoned his daughters are fined.","ideas":["The 'maimed royalty' oracle read as bastardy, not lameness","Agesilaus' jealousy makes his benefactor a carver of meats","A plan to make the kingship elective by merit","Death at Haliartus fulfils the oracle of the Hoplites"],"terms":[{"t":"Heracleidae","d":"Descendants of Heracles; only the Eurypontid and Agiad houses supplied Spartan kings."}]},{"i":240,"j":254,"loc":"Life of Sulla, chs. 1–6","title":"Sulla's origins, temperament, and faith in Fortune","summary":"Sulla comes of an obscure patrician line and lives in cheap lodgings in youth; his blotched face, love of actors and jesting, and inheritances from Nicopolis and his stepmother are described. As Marius' quaestor he receives Jugurtha from Bocchus and flaunts it on his seal-ring, beginning his feud with Marius; he later serves under Catulus, wins the praetorship, restores Ariobarzanes, and first receives a Parthian envoy. Plutarch dwells on Sulla's belief that Fortune and dreams guided him and on his uneven character. He becomes consul at fifty and marries Metella.","ideas":["The surrender of Jugurtha starts the Marius–Sulla rivalry","Sulla credits his successes to Fortune rather than to planning","A Chaldaean predicts he will become the greatest of men","Contradictory character: rapacious yet generous, cruel yet forgiving"],"terms":[{"t":"Tychē / Fortuna","d":"Fortune, the divine power Sulla claimed as the true author of his deeds."}]},{"i":255,"j":262,"loc":"Life of Sulla, chs. 7–10","title":"Portents, Sulpicius, and the march on Rome","summary":"Marius covets the Mithridatic command, and prodigies, including a trumpet from a clear sky that Etruscan seers read as the turn of an age, foretell civil strife. Through the tribune Sulpicius, Marius has the command transferred to himself; Sulla escapes to his army at Nola. Encouraged by a dream of a goddess handing him a thunderbolt, he marches on Rome, deceives the senate's envoys, and enters with fire. Marius flees, but Sulla's harshness and the people's hostility follow; he lets Cinna become consul under oath, and Cinna breaks it as Sulla leaves for the East.","ideas":["Etruscan doctrine of eight ages ended by a great year","Sulpicius' crimes and his law giving Marius the command","Sulla's dream and his torch-lit entry into Rome","Cinna's broken oath with the thrown stone"],"terms":[{"t":"iustitium","d":"Suspension of public business, decreed by the consuls to block Sulpicius' laws."}]},{"i":263,"j":268,"loc":"Life of Sulla, chs. 11–14","title":"The siege and sack of Athens","summary":"Mithridates is at the height of his power, with Archelaüs master of the sea and Bruttius Sura briefly checking him in Boeotia. Sulla besieges Athens and the Piraeus, cutting down the Academy and Lyceum and seizing the treasures of Epidaurus, Olympia, and Delphi; Plutarch contrasts him with earlier Roman generals and blames him for buying armies. The tyrant Aristion revels while the city starves and mocks Sulla and Metella. Learning of a weak spot at the Heptachalcum, Sulla breaks in at midnight; the market-place runs with blood until he spares the living for the sake of the dead, then takes the acropolis and Piraeus.","ideas":["Plunder of Greek sanctuaries to pay for the siege","Generals who court soldiers make their country a thing for sale","Aristion's mockery and the city's famine","The massacre and Sulla's pardon for the sake of the ancient Athenians"],"terms":[{"t":"Heptachalcum","d":"An unguarded stretch of the Athenian wall where Sulla's men got in."}]},{"i":269,"j":279,"loc":"Life of Sulla, chs. 15–21","title":"Victories at Chaeroneia and Orchomenus","summary":"Sulla moves into Boeotia, joins Hortensius, and keeps his frightened men digging until they demand battle, then seizes Parapotamii; Plutarch notes the rescue of his own native Chaeroneia. Guided by two Chaeroneians, troops rout the enemy on Thurium, and in the plain the scythed chariots fail for lack of run while Sulla shuttles between wings; he claims only fourteen men lost and dedicates trophies to Mars, Victory and Venus. Against Dorylaüs' new army at Orchomenus, Sulla rallies fleeing troops by seizing a standard and shaming them, and the enemy camp is stormed with the marshes filled with dead.","ideas":["Hard labour used to make soldiers eager for battle","Scythed chariots rendered useless by a short charge","'Remember to tell them, at Orchomenus'","Plutarch's local knowledge of the Chaeroneia battlefield"],"terms":[]},{"i":280,"j":292,"loc":"Life of Sulla, chs. 22–27","title":"Peace with Mithridates and return to Italy","summary":"With his partisans persecuted by Cinna and Carbo, Sulla negotiates with Archelaüs at Delium, scorning his offer of help and dictating terms; his kindness to Archelaüs later breeds suspicion. Mithridates balks, then meets Sulla at Dardanus and accepts after a rebuke; Fimbria's army deserts and Fimbria kills himself, and Asia is heavily fined. Back in Athens Sulla takes Apellicon's library of Aristotle and Theophrastus, treats his gout at Aedepsus, and is shown a captured satyr. His soldiers swear loyalty, portents attend his landing, and early victories over Norbanus and at Fidentia follow.","ideas":["Sulla refuses to ally with Mithridates' general against Rome","'Suppliants speak first': the meeting at Dardanus","Fate of Aristotle's writings via Tyrannio and Andronicus","Omens of laurel and garlands herald civil-war victories"],"terms":[]},{"i":293,"j":300,"loc":"Life of Sulla, chs. 28–32","title":"Civil war, the Colline Gate, and proscriptions","summary":"Sulla subverts Scipio's army by negotiation, prompting Carbo's remark that the fox in him was worse than the lion, and defeats the younger Marius, who escapes into Praeneste. The Samnite Telesinus nearly takes Rome; at the Colline Gate Sulla's left wing breaks after he prays to his Apollo image, while Crassus wins on the right. Sulla has six thousand prisoners slaughtered while addressing the senate, and Plutarch asks whether power changes or reveals character. The proscriptions follow, rewarding informers and killing men for their property, and twelve thousand are massacred at Praeneste.","ideas":["The fox and the lion in Sulla","Near-disaster at the Colline Gate","Massacre heard from the senate in the temple of Bellona","Proscriptions driven by greed: 'my Alban estate is prosecuting me'"],"terms":[{"t":"proscriptio (proscription)","d":"Public listing of men who could be killed for reward, their property confiscated and their descendants disfranchised."}]},{"i":301,"j":312,"loc":"Life of Sulla, chs. 33–38","title":"Dictatorship, abdication, and Sulla's death","summary":"Sulla revives the dictatorship with unlimited powers, distributes confiscated wealth to favourites, forces marriages, and has Ofella killed in the forum. His triumph is graced by restored exiles, and he takes the name Felix. He lays down office and walks the forum as a private man, warning Pompey about Lepidus. After Metella's death, which he handles with legalistic superstition, he marries Valeria and lives among actors until a worm-ridden disease and a final burst of rage kill him. Pompey secures his funeral, which the rain spares until the pyre is done.","ideas":["Absolute power voted retroactively and for the future","Surnames Felix and Epaphroditus proclaim his faith in Fortune","Voluntary abdication and walking unguarded in the forum","His epitaph: no friend kinder, no enemy more harmful"],"terms":[{"t":"dictator","d":"Emergency office with supreme authority, revived by Sulla after some 120 years."},{"t":"Felix","d":"Latin for 'fortunate', the surname Sulla ordered for himself."}]},{"i":313,"j":326,"loc":"Comparison of Lysander and Sulla","title":"Comparison of Lysander and Sulla","summary":"Plutarch judges that Lysander rose lawfully in a well-governed Sparta, while Sulla seized power in a corrupt Rome and kept it by arms. Lysander's planned reform was milder and arguably just, since merit rather than birth should rule; his crimes served his friends, whereas Sulla killed his. Lysander lived soberly but corrupted Sparta with wealth, while Sulla legislated sobriety and lived licentiously. In war Sulla far surpasses Lysander, who died like a skirmisher at Haliartus; Sulla's refusal to deal with Mithridates while exiled is his finest act. The verdict: Sulla had more successes, Lysander fewer faults.","ideas":["First man of a sick state is not proven the best","Choose rulers like dogs and horses, by quality not pedigree","Lysander's self-control versus Sulla's generalship","Sulla freed Athens; Lysander handed it to tyrants"],"terms":[{"t":"synkrisis (comparison)","d":"The closing set-piece in which Plutarch weighs the paired Lives against each other."}]},{"i":327,"j":348,"loc":"Life of Cimon, chs. 1–6","title":"Damon, Lucullus' testimony, and Cimon's youth","summary":"The Life begins in Chaeroneia: the youth Damon kills a Roman officer who had pursued him, the town is prosecuted by the Orchomenians, and Lucullus' truthful testimony saves it, so Plutarch repays him by writing his Life truthfully, treating faults as shortcomings rather than baseness. Cimon is chosen as his pair for their eastern wars and lavish hospitality. Son of Miltiades and a Thracian princess, Cimon starts with a reputation for drinking and scandal about his sister Elpinicé. At Salamis he dedicates his bridle to Athena, and with Aristides' support he wins the allies from the arrogant Pausanias, whose killing of Cleonicé haunts him.","ideas":["A portrait should neither omit nor stress small blemishes","Lucullus and Cimon paired as warriors abroad and gentle statesmen at home","The dedicated bridle: Athens needs sea-fighters, not cavalry","Leadership of Hellas passes from Pausanias by character, not force"],"terms":[{"t":"Asbolomeni ('Besooted')","d":"Name of Damon's descendants, from the soot he smeared on his face before the murder."}]},{"i":349,"j":380,"loc":"Life of Cimon, chs. 7–10","title":"Eïon, Scyros, Theseus' bones, and Cimon's generosity","summary":"Cimon takes Eïon from the Persians and is honoured with inscribed Hermae, which Plutarch quotes. He seizes Scyros, clears the Aegean of Dolopian pirates, and brings the bones of Theseus home, winning popular favour; as judge he awards Sophocles his first victory. Ion recalls his singing at dinner and his clever division of captives at Sestos and Byzantium. Cimon opens his fields and table to the poor and has his companions give clothes and coins, a 'golden age' generosity that his aristocratic, pro-Spartan politics and refusal of bribes show was not demagoguery.","ideas":["Offensive victories that win new land for Athens","Return of Theseus' bones after about four hundred years","Captives versus ornaments: the ransom stratagem","Generosity likened to the age of Cronus"],"terms":[{"t":"Hermae","d":"Stone pillars with a head of Hermes, set up with verses honouring the victors of Eïon."}]},{"i":381,"j":394,"loc":"Life of Cimon, chs. 11–15","title":"Eurymedon, empire, and the fight over the Areopagus","summary":"Cimon lets allies pay money and send empty ships instead of serving, so Athenians do the fighting and the allies become tributary subjects. He drives the Persians from Asia, bringing Phaselis to terms, then wins a double victory by sea and land at the Eurymedon and destroys the Phoenician reinforcements; Plutarch weighs the evidence for the Peace of Callias. Spoils pay for the Acropolis wall, the Long Walls' foundations, and the Academy groves. After conquering the Chersonese and Thasos he is tried for bribery and acquitted, but while he is away Ephialtes and Pericles strip the Areopagus, and his attempt to restore it revives slanders.","ideas":["Allies' ease turns them into subjects of Athens","Two victories in one day at the Eurymedon","Disputed treaty keeping Persia from the Greek coast","Radical democracy advances when Cimon is abroad"],"terms":[{"t":"Areiopagus (Areopagus)","d":"The ancient council whose judicial powers Ephialtes removed and Cimon tried to restore."}]},{"i":395,"j":411,"loc":"Life of Cimon, chs. 16–19","title":"Sparta's earthquake, ostracism, recall, and death","summary":"Cimon's love of Sparta, shown in his sons' names, turns the Athenians against him. After the great earthquake and the Helot revolt he persuades Athens to send aid, but the Spartans dismiss the Athenians from Ithomé and Cimon is ostracised. Barred from fighting at Tanagra, he urges his friends to clear themselves, and a hundred die; Pericles proposes his recall. He makes peace with Sparta and sails for Cyprus and Egypt despite ominous dreams and signs, and dies besieging Citium, his death concealed for thirty days. Plutarch closes by saying no later Greek general matched his success against the Barbarians.","ideas":["'Do not let Hellas be lamed' and lose its yoke-fellow","Ostracism after the rebuff at Ithomé","Political enmities then were moderate and yielded to the public good","Ammon's reply: Cimon is already with the god"],"terms":[{"t":"ostrakismos (ostracism)","d":"Ten-year banishment by popular vote, imposed on Cimon after the Ithomé affair."},{"t":"Helots","d":"The serf population of Laconia and Messenia who rose against Sparta after the earthquake."}]},{"i":412,"j":425,"loc":"Life of Lucullus, chs. 1–4","title":"Lucullus' family, learning and service under Sulla","summary":"Plutarch opens with Lucullus' mixed family background and his youthful prosecution of his father's accuser, Servilius the Augur. He praises Lucullus' fluency in Latin and Greek, his love of liberal culture and philosophy, and his devotion to his brother Marcus. Under Sulla in the Mithridatic war he runs the mint, then sails in winter through hostile seas to gather ships from Crete, Cyrene, Egypt and Rhodes, refusing Ptolemy's lavish gifts. He declines Fimbria's offer to trap Mithridates at Pitane, defeats the royal fleets, collects Sulla's levy on Asia mildly, and subdues Mitylene. Sulla's dedication of his memoirs and guardianship of his son to Lucullus begins the rivalry with Pompey.","ideas":["Early impeachment of Servilius wins Lucullus fame","Lucullus restores order at Cyrene, recalling Plato's remark on lawgiving for the prosperous","His refusal to help Fimbria lets Mithridates escape by sea","Sulla's favour sows jealousy between Lucullus and Pompey"],"terms":[{"t":"Lucullean coinage","d":"The money Lucullus minted for Sulla's army in Peloponnesus, long current afterwards."},{"t":"Fimbrians","d":"The lawless but hardy troops who had followed Fimbria in murdering the consul Flaccus."}]},{"i":426,"j":440,"loc":"Life of Lucullus, chs. 5–11","title":"Winning the Mithridatic command and relieving Cyzicus","summary":"As consul Lucullus fears Pompey's rise and, against his nature, courts the courtesan Praecia to win Cethegus and so the province of Cilicia, which brings him the war against Mithridates. He disciplines the corrupted Roman army in Asia, while Mithridates returns with a reformed, Roman-style force and Cotta is beaten and shut up in Chalcedon. Lucullus chooses to relieve Cotta, then, judging the king's supplies by questioning captives, shadows his huge army and starves it as it besieges Cyzicus. Omens and a storm aid the Cyzicenes; Mithridates' army, reduced to cannibalism, is cut down at the Rhyndacus and the Granicus.","ideas":["Lucullus gains his command through Praecia's influence over Cethegus","Mithridates rebuilds his army on Roman lines","Lucullus wins by cutting off food rather than by battle","Divine signs at Cyzicus: the heifer, Persephone's dream, Athena at Ilium"],"terms":[{"t":"Cyzicus","d":"City on the Propontis whose siege by Mithridates Lucullus broke by starving the besiegers."}]},{"i":441,"j":456,"loc":"Life of Lucullus, chs. 12–19","title":"Pursuit into Pontus, Cabira and the fall of Amisus","summary":"Prompted by a dream of Aphrodite, Lucullus destroys royal squadrons at sea and captures Sertorius' general Marius; Mithridates loses much of his fleet in a storm. Lucullus invades Pontus, defending his slow advance against critics by arguing that a hasty push would drive the king into the arms of Tigranes. At Cabira he survives an assassination attempt by the Dandarian Olthacus, and Mithridates' camp breaks up in panic; the king escapes only because soldiers stop to plunder his gold. Mithridates has his sisters and wives killed at Pharnacia, Monimé's death being told at length. Amisus is burned and sacked by Lucullus' soldiers against his orders, and he weeps and rebuilds it.","ideas":["Lucullus' strategic reasoning about Tigranes","Sleep saves Lucullus from Olthacus","Soldiers' greed lets Mithridates escape","The sack of Amisus: Lucullus envies Sulla's power to spare Athens"],"terms":[{"t":"Tigranes","d":"King of Armenia, styled King of Kings, and son-in-law of Mithridates."}]},{"i":457,"j":467,"loc":"Life of Lucullus, chs. 20–23","title":"Relief of Asia and embassy to Tigranes","summary":"Lucullus frees the cities of Asia from crushing debt to Roman tax-gatherers and money-lenders by capping interest and limiting creditors' claims, earning their love and the lenders' enmity at Rome. Appius Clodius, sent to demand Mithridates, finds Tigranes swollen with pride, attended by kings like servants, and boldly threatens war; Tigranes refuses. Tigranes now receives Mithridates, and the deaths of Metrodorus of Scepsis and Amphicrates at his court are recounted. Lucullus holds festivals at Ephesus, takes Sinope from its Cilician garrison, and finds the statue of the founder Autolycus, as a dream had foretold.","ideas":["Debt reform: one per cent monthly, no interest beyond principal, a quarter of income","Tigranes' arrogance and his quarrel over titles","Dreams as trustworthy signs, after Sulla's memoirs"],"terms":[{"t":"Lucullea","d":"Festivals the grateful cities of Asia held in Lucullus' honour."}]},{"i":468,"j":484,"loc":"Life of Lucullus, chs. 24–29","title":"Invasion of Armenia and victory at Tigranocerta","summary":"With a small army Lucullus crosses the Euphrates, which falls conveniently, and a sacred heifer offers itself for sacrifice. Tigranes, who has beheaded the first bearer of bad news, learns late of the invasion; his generals are beaten and he gathers a vast host while Lucullus besieges Tigranocerta. Ignoring Mithridates' advice to avoid battle, Tigranes mocks the Romans as too many for envoys and too few for soldiers. On an unlucky day Lucullus routs the mail-clad horsemen from a hill and the huge army collapses with tiny Roman loss. He takes Tigranocerta, sends its transplanted peoples home, and wins the Gordyeni by honouring their murdered king Zarbienus.","ideas":["Taxiles explains that unsheathed armour means battle","Lucullus' famous reply about making a black day lucky","Victory by speed over Tigranes, by delay over Mithridates","Generosity wins over Arabs, Sopheni and Gordyeni"],"terms":[{"t":"cataphracts (mail-clad horsemen)","d":"Heavily armoured cavalry relying wholly on the long spear, vulnerable only at legs and thighs."}]},{"i":485,"j":498,"loc":"Life of Lucullus, chs. 30–36","title":"Mutiny, reverses and supersession by Pompey","summary":"Lucullus plans to strike at Parthia, but his troops in Pontus and Gordyene refuse service, so he turns on Tigranes, wins at the Arsanias and marches on Artaxata, the capital founded on Hannibal's advice. Early winter and mutiny force him back; he takes Nisibis. Plutarch now marks the turn of his fortune, blaming his aloofness toward his soldiers and equals, the demagogues at Rome, and his brother-in-law Publius Clodius, who stirs up the Fimbrians. After Triarius' defeat the army deserts him; Pompey receives the command, annuls his arrangements and strips him of his troops. Plutarch judges that Lucullus' victories later lured Crassus to disaster in Parthia.","ideas":["Lucullus lacked the art of winning soldiers' affection","Clodius' agitation among the Fimbrian troops","The meeting with Pompey and the withered laurel","His conquests tempted Crassus to attack Parthia"],"terms":[{"t":"Artaxata","d":"Armenian royal city said to have been planned by Hannibal for King Artaxas."}]},{"i":499,"j":508,"loc":"Life of Lucullus, chs. 37–43","title":"Triumph, luxurious retirement, library and death","summary":"At Rome Memmius tries to deny Lucullus a triumph, which he wins only through the nobles' efforts; the procession and spoils are described. He divorces Clodia, marries and dismisses Servilia, and withdraws from public life, a choice some praise and Pompey and Crassus mock. Plutarch likens his later life to the second half of an old comedy: costly villas, gardens and banquets, with anecdotes such as Lucullus dining with Lucullus and the dinner in the Apollo. He praises Lucullus' open library and his support of the Old Academy under Antiochus of Ascalon. Lucullus still opposes Pompey's measures, is falsely accused through Vettius, and dies with his mind failing.","ideas":["Contrast of an active first life with a luxurious second","Tubero calls him Xerxes in a toga","His library as a home for Greek scholars","Friendship with Cicero, who named a dialogue Lucullus"],"terms":[{"t":"Old Academy","d":"The school of Antiochus of Ascalon, which Lucullus favoured over Philo's New Academy."},{"t":"katalēpsis (apprehension)","d":"The doctrine of certain knowledge defended by Lucullus in Cicero's dialogue."}]},{"i":509,"j":515,"loc":"Comparison of Cimon and Lucullus","title":"Comparison of Cimon and Lucullus","summary":"Plutarch counts both men fortunate to die before their states fell, though Lucullus' retirement into pleasure suits an Epicurean more than an Academic, and Cimon improved with age while Lucullus declined. Cimon spent his wealth on public works and a generous table, Lucullus on private luxury. Cimon wins the palm for single-day victories by land and sea and for giving Athens its leadership with the allies' goodwill, whereas Lucullus was deserted by his own soldiers. Yet Lucullus was the greater general, crossing the Taurus and the Tigris, facing larger and undefeated foes, and leaving his enemies too weak to resist Pompey. Plutarch finds the verdict hard and judges both favoured by the gods.","ideas":["Change for the better shows the sounder nature","Public versus private use of wealth","Aristocratic natures vex the multitude like physicians' bandages","The general who hands on a weakened foe is greater"],"terms":[]},{"i":516,"j":556,"loc":"Life of Nicias, chs. 1–6","title":"Nicias' character, piety and cautious leadership","summary":"Plutarch pairs Nicias with Crassus, disclaims rivalry with Thucydides, criticises Timaeus' pedantry, and promises to gather lesser-known details that reveal character. Nicias, ranked by Aristotle among Athens' best citizens, leads the rich against Cleon and wins the people by his very timidity and by lavish choruses and offerings, including the bridge of boats at Delos and the bronze palm-tree. Deeply superstitious, he keeps a diviner, pays off informers, and is mocked by comic poets for cowardice. He avoids long commands, credits his successes to fortune, and wins at Cythera, Minoa, Nisaea and Corinth, giving up a trophy to recover two unburied dead.","ideas":["The people exalt those who fear them","Liturgies and dedications as means of popularity and piety","Comic poets on Nicias' fear and bribery of informers","Recovering two dead outweighs the honour of victory"],"terms":[{"t":"choregia","d":"Funding of a public chorus, by which Nicias won repeated victories and favour."}]},{"i":557,"j":579,"loc":"Life of Nicias, chs. 7–10","title":"Pylos, Cleon, and the Peace of Nicias","summary":"When the siege of the Spartans on Sphacteria drags on, Cleon blames Nicias, who resigns the command to him; Cleon succeeds within his promised twenty days, to Nicias' great discredit and Athens' harm. After Cleon and Brasidas fall at Amphipolis, Nicias persuades both cities to make peace, which bears his name, and secures the first restitution by Sparta by buying the lot. Alcibiades, slighted by Sparta, tricks the Spartan envoys into denying their full powers before the assembly and discredits them. Nicias' embassy to Sparta fails, and Athens allies with Argos and returns to war.","ideas":["Nicias' resignation at Pylos seen as worse than throwing away a shield","Cleon's coarse style corrupts public speaking","The Peace of Nicias as the counterpart of Pericles' war","Alcibiades' deception of the Spartan ambassadors"],"terms":[{"t":"Peace of Nicias","d":"The truce between Athens and Sparta credited to Nicias' efforts."}]},{"i":580,"j":597,"loc":"Life of Nicias, chs. 11–13","title":"Ostracism of Hyperbolus and the Sicilian vote","summary":"The feud of Nicias and Alcibiades leads to an ostracism, but the two combine their factions and banish the demagogue Hyperbolus instead, the last man ever ostracised. Plutarch reflects that Nicias would have fared better had he risked the vote. When Egesta and Leontini seek aid, Alcibiades fires Athens with dreams of Sicily and Carthage; Nicias opposes the expedition but is elected general with full powers alongside Alcibiades and Lamachus. Many omens are disregarded: the mutilation of the Hermae, the ravens at Delphi, the priestess named Peace, Meton's ruse, Socrates' sign, and the rites of Adonis.","ideas":["Ostracism degraded by its use on Hyperbolus","Nicias' opposition only confirms his appointment","Ill omens before the expedition sails"],"terms":[{"t":"ostrakismos (ostracism)","d":"Athenian vote exiling a man for ten years, ended after Hyperbolus."},{"t":"Hermae","d":"Pillar-statues of Hermes, all but one mutilated in a single night before the expedition."}]},{"i":598,"j":613,"loc":"Life of Nicias, chs. 14–18","title":"Hesitation, early successes, and walling Syracuse","summary":"Plutarch faults Nicias for timidity once the expedition sailed: he proposes a mere display voyage, and after Alcibiades' recall wastes time in idle cruising. He draws the Syracusans out to Catana by a false message, seizes a camp near Syracuse and wins a battle, but spares the Olympieum and winters at Naxos. Returning, he takes Epipolae and nearly completes a wall around the city despite kidney disease. Lamachus dies in single combat, Nicias saves the walls from his sickbed with fire, and as sole general he grows overconfident, ignoring the approach of Gylippus.","ideas":["Vigorous in action but hesitant to begin","The Catana stratagem as his best generalship","Sophocles' quip on age and seniority","Overconfidence lets Gylippus land unobserved"],"terms":[{"t":"Epipolae","d":"The heights above Syracuse that Nicias seized for his siege works."}]},{"i":614,"j":626,"loc":"Life of Nicias, chs. 19–22","title":"Gylippus' arrival and the night battle on Epipolae","summary":"Gongylus and then Gylippus arrive just as Syracuse is about to treat for terms; Gylippus offers the Athenians safe conduct, changes his tactics after a first defeat, and runs a counter-wall across the Athenian lines. Nicias asks Athens for reinforcements or recall. Plemmyrium is lost, and the new colleagues Menander and Euthydemus force a sea battle and lose. Demosthenes arrives in splendour, overrules Nicias' counsel of delay, and makes a night attack on Epipolae that ends in moonlit confusion and two thousand dead. Nicias, fearing Athenian prosecution more than the enemy, resists withdrawal until sickness forces him to agree.","ideas":["Gylippus' cloak and staff as symbols of Sparta's majesty","The counter-wall nullifies Athenian superiority","Moonlight deceives the attackers at Epipolae","Nicias would rather die by enemies than by fellow citizens"],"terms":[]},{"i":627,"j":647,"loc":"Life of Nicias, chs. 23–30","title":"The eclipse, the retreat, and the Sicilian catastrophe","summary":"A lunar eclipse terrifies Nicias, who lacks a skilled seer and delays a full month; Plutarch digresses on Anaxagoras and the suspicion of natural philosophers until Plato's repute. The Syracusans close the harbour and win the great sea battle, and Hermocrates' false message delays the retreat. Sick and starving, Nicias leads the march with dignity, but Demosthenes' division is captured and at the Asinarus the army is slaughtered; Nicias surrenders to Gylippus. The Syracusans vote to kill the generals and confine the free captives in the quarries, where most die, though some win freedom by reciting Euripides. Athens at first racks the man who brings the news.","ideas":["Superstition over the eclipse seals the army's fate","Nicias' plea to Gylippus at the Asinarus","Hermocrates urges a noble use of victory and is shouted down","Captives saved by knowing Euripides"],"terms":[{"t":"Asinaria","d":"Syracusan festival decreed for the day Nicias was taken at the Asinarus."},{"t":"latomiai (stone quarries)","d":"The quarries where the free Athenian captives were held and mostly perished."}]},{"i":648,"j":666,"loc":"Life of Crassus, chs. 1–9","title":"Crassus's avarice, youth, and Spartacus's revolt","summary":"Plutarch introduces Marcus Crassus as temperate in private life but ruled by one vice, avarice, which he illustrates from Sulla's confiscations, fire-sale houses, and a household of trained slaves. Crassus flees the Marian terror to Spain, hides eight months in a seaside cave fed by Vibius Paciacus, then joins Sulla, fighting well but staining his name by greed. His rivalry with Pompey turns him to politics, where he wins influence by advocacy, loans, and accessibility, shifting his allegiances as interest dictates. The section closes with the outbreak of the gladiators' war under Spartacus and his early victories over praetors and consuls.","ideas":["Crassus's wealth was gathered from public calamities, fire and proscription","Hiding in the Spanish cave, provisioned by Vibius","Rivalry with Pompey drives Crassus into civic influence rather than war","Spartacus escapes from Capua and defeats successive Roman commanders"],"terms":[{"t":"philoploutia (love of wealth)","d":"The avarice Plutarch names as Crassus's single vice that obscured his other virtues."},{"t":"Imperator","d":"Title of victorious general with which Sulla honoured the young Pompey, to Crassus's vexation."}]},{"i":667,"j":683,"loc":"Life of Crassus, chs. 10–17","title":"Victory over Spartacus and the triumvirate","summary":"Given the command against Spartacus, Crassus revives decimation, walls in the slaves at Rhegium, and finally defeats Spartacus, who dies fighting, though Pompey claims to have ended the war. Crassus shares a quarrelsome consulship with Pompey, is reconciled with him publicly, and has a barren censorship. Caesar unites him with Pompey; after Luca they seize a second consulship by violence and take provinces, Syria falling to Crassus. Elated, Crassus dreams of conquests to India, leaves Rome under the tribune Ateius's curses, and after minor gains in Mesopotamia winters in Syria counting temple treasure.","ideas":["Decimation and the wall across the Rhegium isthmus","Death of Spartacus; Pompey takes the credit; Crassus gets only an ovation","The coalition of Caesar, Pompey and Crassus, renewed at Luca","Ateius's curses and the omen at Hierapolis"],"terms":[{"t":"decimation","d":"Execution of one man in ten from a cowardly unit, an ancient penalty Crassus revived."},{"t":"ovation","d":"The minor triumph, celebrated on foot, which Crassus took for the servile war."}]},{"i":684,"j":700,"loc":"Life of Crassus, chs. 18–25","title":"March into the desert and Carrhae","summary":"Crassus scorns the Parthian envoys and his officers' warnings, rejects the Armenian king's advice to go through the mountains, and ignores a string of evil omens at the Euphrates. The Arab chieftain Ariamnes lures him away from the river into open desert, while Plutarch describes Surena, the Parthian commander. At the Balissus the Parthians appear with drums and gleaming armour, surround the Roman square, and shoot it down with arrows from an endless supply. Publius Crassus charges, is drawn off, surrounded on a hillock, and dies with his men; his head is cut off.","ideas":["Omens and Crassus's obstinacy before the campaign","Ariamnes' treacherous guidance into the waterless plain","Parthian horse-archery and the Parthian shot against the hollow square","The heroic death of Publius and his Gallic cavalry"],"terms":[{"t":"cataphracts (mail-clad horsemen)","d":"Parthian heavy cavalry with long pikes who pinned the Romans while archers shot them."},{"t":"Surena","d":"The young Parthian noble and general who commanded against Crassus."}]},{"i":701,"j":724,"loc":"Life of Crassus, chs. 26–33","title":"Retreat, treachery, and the death of Crassus","summary":"The Parthians parade Publius's head; Crassus exhorts his men nobly, but the army is broken and that night abandons its wounded and flees to Carrhae. Betrayed by the guide Andromachus, Crassus is trapped near Sinnaca; his mutinous soldiers force him to accept Surena's offer of a parley, where he is killed in a scuffle. Surena stages a mock triumph in Seleucia, and at the Armenian court the head of Crassus is used in a performance of Euripides' Bacchae. Plutarch ends with the punishment of Hyrodes and Surena.","ideas":["Crassus's speech after his son's death","The night retreat and the abandoned wounded","Forced parley and death at the hands of the Parthians","The head of Crassus in the Bacchae performance"],"terms":[{"t":"Bacchae","d":"Euripides' play, whose scene of Agave carrying Pentheus's head the actor Jason performs with Crassus's head."}]},{"i":725,"j":735,"loc":"Comparison of Nicias and Crassus","title":"Comparison of Nicias and Crassus","summary":"Plutarch judges Nicias's wealth more honestly gained and his politics free of violence, while Crassus was faithless and violent. Yet Nicias is censured for timidity, above all for yielding the Pylos command to Cleon, while Crassus contended against Caesar and Pompey. Nicias wisely opposed the Sicilian venture, whereas Crassus thought Parthia easy, though Plutarch notes that one should not judge a beginning by its end. In their deaths Crassus is less to blame, since Nicias surrendered in hope of a shameful safety.","ideas":["Wealth: mines versus confiscations and fires","Timidity of Nicias against the ambition of Crassus","Athens sent Nicias unwilling; Crassus led Rome unwilling","Crassus's death less disgraceful than Nicias's surrender"],"terms":[{"t":"synkrisis (comparison)","d":"The closing paired assessment Plutarch attaches to each pair of Lives."}]},{"i":736,"j":755,"loc":"Life of Eumenes, chs. 1–9","title":"Eumenes rises and defeats Craterus","summary":"Eumenes of Cardia, Alexander's chief secretary, wins high honour and a Persian wife, though he clashes with Hephaestion and once with Alexander over money. After Alexander's death he receives Cappadocia, escapes Leonnatus's schemes, and serves Perdiccas, raising a native cavalry force. He defeats Neoptolemus and then Craterus, concealing the enemy general's name from his Macedonians; he kills Neoptolemus in hand-to-hand combat and weeps over the dying Craterus. Condemned after Perdiccas's death, he pays his troops by selling them estates to plunder, but is betrayed and beaten by Antigonus at Orcynii, yet buries his dead and spares Antigonus's baggage.","ideas":["A Greek secretary among Macedonian generals","The dream of Athena and Demeter and the watchword 'Demeter and Alexander'","Duel with Neoptolemus; grief over Craterus","Greatness shown in misfortune after Orcynii"],"terms":[{"t":"archigrammateus (chief secretary)","d":"Eumenes's post under Alexander, mocked by Neoptolemus as service with pen and paper."},{"t":"satrapy","d":"A provincial governorship; Eumenes received Cappadocia and Paphlagonia."}]},{"i":756,"j":775,"loc":"Life of Eumenes, chs. 10–19","title":"Siege of Nora, Silver-shields, and betrayal","summary":"Besieged in Nora, Eumenes keeps his men cheerful and exercises his horses by hoisting them on pulleys; he amends Antigonus's oath to swear to Olympias and the kings and escapes. Appointed by Polysperchon against Antigonus, he manages jealous commanders through Alexander's tent and by borrowing their money. The soldiers demand him in battle even when he is carried sick in a litter, and his false campfires deceive Antigonus. After a battle won by the Silver-shields but with their baggage lost, they surrender him to Antigonus; he rebukes them in a speech, is starved and killed, and Antigonus destroys the Silver-shields.","ideas":["Ingenuity at Nora and the corrected oath","Alexander's tent as a device to lead rival commanders","Creditors as bodyguards; the litter and the campfire ruse","Betrayal by the Silver-shields for their baggage"],"terms":[{"t":"Argyraspides (Silver-shields)","d":"Veteran Macedonian infantry of Philip and Alexander who betrayed Eumenes."}]},{"i":776,"j":792,"loc":"Life of Sertorius, chs. 1–10","title":"Sertorius's early career and exile","summary":"Plutarch opens on coincidences in history, groups Sertorius with one-eyed generals, and pairs him with Eumenes. Sertorius, a Sabine reared by his mother, distinguishes himself against the Cimbri, in Spain, and in the Marsic war, where he loses an eye. He joins Cinna, opposes and later restrains Marius, then leaves for Spain as the Marian cause collapses. Driven out, he wanders by sea, hears of the Islands of the Blest and longs to live there, then fights in Maurusia, opens the tomb of Antaeus, and Plutarch reflects on whether his later cruelty belied his nature.","ideas":["Fortune repeats events in an infinite time","Sertorius's lost eye as a badge of valour","The Islands of the Blest and his wish for peace","Virtue grounded in reason cannot become its opposite"],"terms":[{"t":"Islands of the Blest","d":"Atlantic islands with mild climate, believed to be Homer's Elysian Field."},{"t":"tychē (fortune)","d":"The changing power Plutarch says Sertorius found harder than his enemies."}]},{"i":793,"j":809,"loc":"Life of Sertorius, chs. 11–20","title":"Leader of the Lusitanians against Metellus and Pompey","summary":"Invited by the Lusitanians, Sertorius wins Spain by mildness and cunning, chiefly through a white doe he claims is a gift of Diana. With small forces he outmanoeuvres Metellus by guerrilla tactics, relieves Langobritae, educates noble Iberian boys at Osca, and is joined by Perpenna's troops. He teaches perseverance with the horse-tail demonstration and subdues the cave-dwelling Characitani by blowing dust into their caves. Against Pompey he takes Lauron under his eyes and wins at the Sucro until Metellus arrives, and the recovered doe restores his followers' confidence.","ideas":["The white doe as a religious device","Guerrilla warfare against the heavy Roman phalanx","The two horses' tails: persistence over force","Lauron and the Sucro against Pompey"],"terms":[{"t":"consecration (devotio)","d":"Iberian custom by which a leader's companions vow to die with him."}]},{"i":810,"j":824,"loc":"Life of Sertorius, chs. 21–27; Comparison of Eumenes and Sertorius","title":"Sertorius's murder and the comparison with Eumenes","summary":"Sertorius fights at Saguntum, outwits the besiegers, and reduces Metellus and Pompey to want; he keeps a Roman senate, refuses Mithridates the province of Asia, and longs to return home. Jealous Roman exiles led by Perpenna provoke revolts, Sertorius turns harsh to the hostages at Osca, and he is stabbed at a banquet. Perpenna is beaten by Pompey, who burns Sertorius's papers unread and executes him. Plutarch then compares the two exiles: Eumenes loved war and power and died begging, Sertorius sought peace and died blamelessly through trusting friends.","ideas":["Sertorius's loyalty to Rome and to his mother","Refusal to cede Asia to Mithridates","The banquet murder by Perpenna; Pompey burns the letters","Eumenes fond of war, Sertorius warlike for safety"],"terms":[{"t":"Osca","d":"City where Sertorius educated, and in effect held hostage, the sons of Iberian nobles."}]}]});